  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "Four families of light names",
      "content": "<p>Most of the names here grow from a handful of words. Latin <em>lux</em> gives Lucius, and from him Lucy, Lucia, Lucie and Lucian. Arabic <em>nur</em> gives Nur, Noor, Nour, Nura and Nuri. Hebrew <em>or</em> appears in Or, Ori, Ora, Orli, Lior and, as <em>ur</em>, in Uri and Uriel. Sanskrit <em>dipa</em>, 'lamp', ends many Sikh names such as Kuldeep, Navdeep and Amandeep.</p><p>If you want a name that carries the meaning clearly, pick from these families. If you want something less obvious, look at Svetlana, Drita or Säde.</p>"
    },
    {
      "heading": "Spelling choices",
      "content": "<p>Nur, Noor and Nour are the same Arabic name written three ways, and families choose by region and habit as much as anything. Noor is the most common in England and Wales. With Hebrew names, the 'my' ending (Ori, Uri, Nuri in Arabic) is part of the meaning, so Ori is 'my light' rather than just 'light'.</p>"
    },
    {
      "heading": "Nicknames and short forms",
      "content": "<p>Lucia and Lucinda both shorten to Lucy or Lu, and Lucian to Luke, which is handy if you like Luke but want a name with a firmer link to light. Uriel can become Uri, and Svetlana is Sveta at home in Russian.</p>"
    }
  ],
  "faqs": [
    {
      "question": "What girl names mean light?",
      "answer": "Lucy, Lucia and Lucie come from the Latin word for light. Others include Noor and Nura (Arabic), Ora and Orli (Hebrew), Diya and Roshni (Hindi), Svetlana (Russian) and Luz (Spanish)."
    },
    {
      "question": "What name means light of God?",
      "answer": "Uriel means 'God is my light' and Orel 'light of God' in Hebrew. Uriah means 'Yahweh is my light', and Neriah 'lamp of Yahweh'."
    },
    {
      "question": "What name means light in Hebrew?",
      "answer": "Or, and names built on it: Ori and Uri ('my light'), Ora, Orit, Orli ('light for me'), Lior ('my light'), Meir ('giving light') and Zohar ('light, brilliance')."
    }
  ],
